    <statement><p>Find the volume of the region enclosed by <m>z=1-y^2</m> and <m>z=y^2-1</m> for <m>0\le x \le 60</m>.</p>
<p><m>V=</m> <fillin characters="20" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>160</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><image xmlns:pi="http://pretextbook.org/2020/pretext/internal" pi:generated="webwork/images/Library__WHFreeman__Rogawski_Calculus_Early_Transcendentals_Second_Edition__15_Multiple_Integration__15__2_Double_Integrals_over_More_General_Regions__15__2__46__pg-image-1.png" width="40%"/>



The volume of the region is the double integral of <m>f( y,z )=60</m> over the domain <m>\mathcal{D}</m> in the <m>yz</m>-plane between the curves <m>z=1-{y}^2</m> and <m>z={y}^2-1</m>.



<image xmlns:pi="http://pretextbook.org/2020/pretext/internal" pi:generated="webwork/images/Library__WHFreeman__Rogawski_Calculus_Early_Transcendentals_Second_Edition__15_Multiple_Integration__15__2_Double_Integrals_over_More_General_Regions__15__2__46__pg-image-2.png" width="36%"/>



This domain is the vertically simple region described by the inequalities
<me>-1\le y\le 1,\quad {y}^2-1\le z\le 1-{y}^2</me>
We compute the double integral as the following iterated integral:
<me>\begin{array}{rcl}
V =
\int\!\!\int _{\mathcal{D}} 60 \,dA \amp =\amp 
\int_{-1}^1 \int_{{y}^2-1}^{1-{y}^2}60 \,dz \,dy\\
\amp =\amp 
\int_{-1}^1 60 z\bigg|_{z={y}^2-1}^{1-{y}^2}dy=
\int_{-1}^1 60\left((1-{y}^2)-({y}^2-1)\right) \,dy\\
\amp =\amp 

\int_{-1}^1 (120-120{y}^2) \,dy=
\int_0^1 (240-240{y}^2) \,dy\\
\amp =\amp 
240 y-\frac{240}{3} y^3\bigg|_0^1=
240 -\frac{240}{3}\\
\amp =\amp 
\frac{480}{3}=160
\end{array}</me>








</solution>
